The people who don't wish for artifacts presumably don't need to be reminded 
that they don't wish for artifacts. 



You should probably distinguish between items that are high priority early 
wishes, and those that are late-game wishes.

Note that *DSM can be obtained by reverse genocide or even from a random 
dragon, with luck.

Gauntlets of dexterity are not a high priority wish at all; they are only a 
little better than leather gloves. 

Late-game, spellbook of polymorph is a high-priority wish for a spellcaster.

I think 2 blessed potions of gain ability is a horrible wish since you can 
get them from alchemy fairly easily.

Magicbane is worth enchanting over +2, since it boosts damage.

Mojo and Stormbringer simply are not in the same league as Grayswandir, and 
even Frost Brand is not quite as good.  

You only want to wish for a T-shirt if you've found/polypiled none, and have 
managed to enchant your other 5-6 pieces of armor to +4/+5, and weapon(s) to 
+6/+7.  That in my mind pushes its priority so far down that it belongs only 
on the list of things to wish when you find three wands of wishing in one 
game.

You can and should wish for certain items greased.

I'd use the word "fixed" instead of fooproof, and "holy" instead of 
"blessed", since they are easier to type.

(At the risk of restarting a Holy War) +3 enchantment and 3 items give the 
highest expected values, so the list should use those instead of +2/2.

> Also, consider inserting the quest artifacts in the appropriate High-
> Med-Low sections. I'd definitely include the PYEC, Eye of Misspelling, 
> and Orb of Fate, maybe the H's staff as well.

Orb of Detection  --  Very heavy, gives MR and telepathy. Low.
Sceptre of Might  --  Gives MR and conflict, so several nice things                                                              
with just one wish.  But it's bad against lawful monsters.  I've        
never used it, but I'd probably rate it Medium early-game, wasted wish late-
game.
Magic Mirror of Merlin  --  MR and telepathy.  It's better than wishing for 
a cloak of magic resistance.  Medium.
Mitre of Holiness  --  Awful.
Tsurugi of Muramasa  --  Wish for Grayswandir instead.

PYEC  --  MR, telepathy, charging.  Medium, because the Eye is better.
Orb of Fate  --  Half damage, level teleport, very heavy.  The Eye is still 
better for casters.  High.
Eye of the Aethiopica  --  MR, energy regeneration, portal.  High for 
casters, otherwise Medium (you can use the extra energy to teleport a lot). 
Heart of Ahriman  --  Awful.
Staff of Aesculapius  --  If you can't two weapon but can get skilled at 
staff it's very good, otherwise wish for Grayswandir.  Medium, because there 
are several other things you want first.
Eyes of the Overworld  --  MR, other abilities.  The other neutral artifacts 
are better.

Longbow of Diana  --  Awful.
Master Key of Thievery  --  TC, half physical damage.  Medium.




Remember, the default value of a wish is one magic marker, which when 
recharged is worth on average a little over 100 charges.  If you can obtain 
something for less than that, you shouldn't wish for it.  Example:  It's far 
better to reverse-genocide tengu (22 marker charges, 1/5 of a wish) than to 
use a whole wish for a ring of of TC. 

In the long run, nearly anything can be obtained by polypiling, but of 
course sometimes you want an item NOW, or you run out of polymorph 
charges/spell energy before you get the item.  Even so, some things are 
fairly easy to polypile for, and should not be medium priority wishes.  
Example: tinning kit is a non-magical tool, so it isn't hard to gather 
enough tools to give a decent chance of getting it.  So I'd rank it Low.

None of the rings are early-game wishes, and late game it's better to wish 
up a wand of polymorph (better yet, write a scroll of charging to recharge 
an existing wand) and zap your excess rings.  Of course you polypile other 
items at the same time...

So I'd put all the rings as low-priority or below.  Exception: ring of 
conflict, which can allow a ridiculously underpowered character to beat the 
castle.

> I'd probably add a Robe as a medium-priority wish for spellcasters. 

I disagree; it can be obtained from a priest.  Add it to the Without Wishing
section, if any.

